<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loi Cadre Defferre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1956</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as loi-cadre Defferre, Overseas Reform Act of 1956</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Loi Cadre Defferre was a French framework law of 23 June 1956, named after the overseas minister Gaston Defferre, that reformed the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s African and Malagasy territories. It introduced universal suffrage on a single electoral roll and gave each territory an elected assembly and a government council. By shifting power to the territories, it weakened the federations of French West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loi-cadre-defferre.jpg" alt="Loi Cadre Defferre" width="280" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sénat français · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaston_Defferre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
